{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate a specific form of technical debt I call 'implicit cross-constraint entanglement,' where locally correct changes subtly create undocumented, load-bearing dependencies between otherwise decoupled modules. The system's true architectural invariants become distributed tacit knowledge that lives only in the heads of aging maintainers, rather than in code, tests, or documentation. Because these constraints are invisible to local analysis, any subsequent change risks violating an invariant it didn't know existed, geometrically increasing the combinatorial space of required pre-checks and causing the marginal cost of change to rise exponentially with system age."}
